## Setup

Each lighthouse has value $x_i \in \{+1, -1\}$. Set $y_i \in \mathbb{F}_2$ with $x_i = (-1)^{y_i}$. The product of any subset $S$ equals $(-1)^{\sum_{i\in S} y_i}$. So each query reveals the parity (mod 2) of the sum of the corresponding $y_i$'s. The target is the parity of $y_1 + \cdots + y_n$.

A set of queries determines the target **iff** the all-ones vector $\mathbf{1} = (1,\dots,1) \in \mathbb{F}_2^n$ lies in the span of the query vectors. The minimum number of queries equals the minimum weight (number of nonzero coefficients) of a vector $\mathbf{c}$ with $\bigoplus c_i \mathbf{v}_i = \mathbf{1}$.

## Part 1: $p(n)$ — Omni-Sensor (any triple)

Each query vector has weight 3. We need $\mathbf{c}$ with $\bigoplus c_i \mathbf{v}_i = \mathbf{1}$, minimizing $k = \mathrm{wt}(\mathbf{c})$. Equivalently: choose $k$ triples so every element appears an **odd** number of times.

**Necessary conditions:** Total appearances $= 3k$. Each of $n$ elements appears an odd number of times $\geq 1$, so $3k \geq n$ (i.e., $k \geq \lceil n/3\rceil$) and $3k \equiv n \pmod{2}$ (i.e., $k \equiv n \pmod{2}$, since 3 is odd).

**Sufficiency (construction):** For the smallest $k \geq \lceil n/3\rceil$ with $k \equiv n\pmod{2}$, write $3k = n + 2a$ where $a = (3k-n)/2$. We need $a$ elements appearing 3 times and $n-a$ appearing once (all odd). This requires $a \leq k$ (an element appearing 3 times must be in 3 distinct triples) and enough slots. One checks this is always feasible for $n \geq 4$ by the explicit construction: pick $a$ "common" elements each placed in 3 triples, distributing the remaining $3k - 3a = n - a$ singleton slots (one per remaining element) across the triples.

